1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:03,767
A message can be a customer question, a sponsorship

2
00:00:03,767 --> 00:00:06,867
offer, or text aimed at the assistant itself.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,900
The inbox cannot tell you which one to trust.

4
00:00:11,267 --> 00:00:15,967
If an assistant treats the email as instructions, the sender may steer it.

5
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:19,500
A subject line, quoted reply, attachment, or

6
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:22,533
link can all contain untrusted content.

7
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:27,667
An assistant with broad mailbox access could act on a bad decision.

8
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:31,600
Skill Y T makes the permitted actions explicit and keeps

9
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,700
the send operation outside the agent boundary.

10
00:00:35,267 --> 00:00:39,000
Skill Y T is an open-source reference project.

11
00:00:39,133 --> 00:00:43,133
It includes a Python application, a reusable Codex skill,

12
00:00:43,133 --> 00:00:46,700
and a separate Node example you can run on its own.

13
00:00:47,267 --> 00:00:49,933
The Python application is the main reference.

14
00:00:50,033 --> 00:00:52,600
Run the command line demo and it builds a tiny

15
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:55,133
synthetic inbox with three distinct messages.

16
00:00:55,267 --> 00:00:57,733
It prints the workflow results as JSON.

17
00:00:58,267 --> 00:01:01,467
The Node acceptance example is separate from Python.

18
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:05,633
Its fixtures illustrate the same safety goals using Node built-ins.

19
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:09,700
The Codex skill takes those principles into another repository.

20
00:01:10,267 --> 00:01:13,267
The agent reads a message, classifies it, and applies

21
00:01:13,267 --> 00:01:15,667
deterministic safeguards before it drafts.

22
00:01:15,767 --> 00:01:19,933
For example, a prompt injection match overrides the classifier’s decision.

23
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:21,700
Policy stays in code.

24
00:01:22,267 --> 00:01:25,100
The first message asks about the recipient’s work.

25
00:01:25,233 --> 00:01:28,633
The offline application creates a simple acknowledgment.

26
00:01:28,767 --> 00:01:30,667
That draft is stored in memory.

27
00:01:30,767 --> 00:01:33,733
A person still decides what to do with the reply.

28
00:01:34,267 --> 00:01:36,667
The second message asks about a sponsorship.

29
00:01:36,767 --> 00:01:39,533
Sponsorship drafting requires approved terms, an

30
00:01:39,533 --> 00:01:41,833
explicit enable flag, and a price floor.

31
00:01:41,933 --> 00:01:44,533
The default Python demo has no configured terms

32
00:01:44,533 --> 00:01:46,700
or floor, so the message goes to review.

33
00:01:47,267 --> 00:01:50,000
The third fixture urges the reader to verify an

34
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,667
account and attempts to override instructions.

35
00:01:52,767 --> 00:01:57,100
The demo detects the prompt injection and creates no draft for that message.

36
00:01:57,233 --> 00:01:59,733
Pattern checks help catch common cases.

37
00:02:00,267 --> 00:02:03,833
The optional Gmail adapter is outside the offline demo.

38
00:02:03,933 --> 00:02:07,867
At the provider level, Compose and Modify scopes can authorize sending.

39
00:02:07,967 --> 00:02:12,700
Inside Skill Y T, the agent gets a narrower interface with no send method.

40
00:02:13,267 --> 00:02:15,933
The agent gets explicitly allowed operations, and the

41
00:02:15,933 --> 00:02:18,400
underlying Gmail client stays inside the adapter.

42
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:21,000
Model instructions also treat email as data.

43
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:24,733
Human review remains the last checkpoint before any real outbound mail.

44
00:02:25,267 --> 00:02:27,633
The demo skips repeat message IDs.

45
00:02:27,767 --> 00:02:31,000
In the optional single worker Gmail flow, draft creation

46
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
stores an opaque marker derived from the message ID.

47
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:37,700
A retry can look up that marker and recover the existing draft.

48
00:02:38,267 --> 00:02:39,800
Labels are workflow state.

49
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:41,700
They provide no transactional lock.

50
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,333
Multiple workers, delayed Gmail visibility, or a

51
00:02:44,333 --> 00:02:46,533
deleted draft can require manual handling.

52
00:02:46,633 --> 00:02:49,733
Prompt injection matching also remains one layer of defense.

53
00:02:50,267 --> 00:02:54,333
The repository includes a skill called Secure Inbox Agent.

54
00:02:54,433 --> 00:02:58,133
Its Skill dot M D file gives Codex a reusable workflow

55
00:02:58,133 --> 00:03:01,000
for building or auditing inbox assistants.

56
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:04,700
It carries design guidance and links to testing references.

57
00:03:05,267 --> 00:03:09,467
The skill directs a developer to trace mailbox operations, verify that

58
00:03:09,467 --> 00:03:13,267
policy decisions happen in code, and inspect recovery behavior.

59
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,733
It calls for synthetic fixtures that exercise uncertainty,

60
00:03:16,733 --> 00:03:19,700
malicious input, duplicates, and prohibited actions.

61
00:03:20,267 --> 00:03:24,000
The installer can copy the skill into a disposable project.

62
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:27,800
Open that project in Codex and invoke the skill by name.

63
00:03:27,933 --> 00:03:31,000
Installation was checked during the project closeout.

64
00:03:31,133 --> 00:03:34,700
A separate model-backed invocation remains unverified.

65
00:03:35,267 --> 00:03:38,367
This excerpt is from the real Python demo.

66
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:40,733
The first ID is drafted.

67
00:03:40,867 --> 00:03:42,700
The next two need review.

68
00:03:42,833 --> 00:03:47,833
The draft count is one and the send operation available flag is false.

69
00:03:49,267 --> 00:03:52,033
The demo runs the same inbox twice.

70
00:03:52,167 --> 00:03:56,267
On the second pass, all three IDs are marked duplicate skipped.

71
00:03:56,367 --> 00:03:59,000
The injection detected flag is true.

72
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:01,733
These results come from synthetic data.

73
00:04:02,267 --> 00:04:07,133
Run npm test and npm run demo in the independent Node example.

74
00:04:07,267 --> 00:04:08,900
Twelve tests passed.

75
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,367
The Node demo creates two pending acknowledgments, holds the

76
00:04:12,367 --> 00:04:15,700
suspicious message without a draft, and skips the duplicate.

77
00:04:16,267 --> 00:04:18,800
Local Python checks passed forty-five tests and

78
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:21,300
skipped the dedicated Docker integration test.

79
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:24,100
GitHub Actions passed Python on two versions, the

80
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:26,833
actual Docker inspector job, and Node acceptance.

81
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:29,700
These runs did not use a live Gmail mailbox.

82
00:04:30,267 --> 00:04:32,633
Clone Skill Y T, create a Python virtual

83
00:04:32,633 --> 00:04:35,033
environment, and install the test extra.

84
00:04:35,133 --> 00:04:36,967
Run the Python command shown here.

85
00:04:37,067 --> 00:04:39,733
The hands-on guide walks through the same sequence.

86
00:04:40,267 --> 00:04:43,300
The testing guide connects the Python demo, the Node

87
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:45,867
suite, and project-local skill installation.

88
00:04:45,967 --> 00:04:49,700
Use your own run output to check the documented behavior.

89
00:04:50,267 --> 00:04:51,933
Skill Y T is open source.

90
00:04:52,033 --> 00:04:54,400
Start with the synthetic demo, inspect the boundaries,

91
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:56,300
and adapt the workflow to your own project.

92
